  "s3": {
    "duration": 25.1,
    "text": "To a computer, each picture is a grid of numbers. Each pixel value counts the light that landed on that pixel during the exposure time. A star is a source: a bump in those counts. Around it, every pixel still collects some light from the sky itself: that's the background. Even two pixels of plain sky don't read exactly the same number, because light arrives as random photons. That's noise.",
