  "s4": {
    "duration": 45.325,
    "text": "But your pictures also differ for a second reason: they weren't all taken the same way. In this course, we call that the observing conditions: not just the sky and the air, but also where the camera points and how long it collects light. The camera may point a little differently. The moon or a thin haze can make the background brighter. You might change the exposure time. And starlight crosses kilometres of moving air, which spreads each star's light over more pixels. Astronomers call this seeing, and it changes from night to night. The shape that one point of light takes in the image, set by the telescope and the seeing, is called the point-spread function, or P S F. So two pictures can differ because the sky really changed, or because the observing conditions did.",
    "words": [
      {
        "w": "But",
        "start": 0.25,
        "end": 0.375
      },
